%{ if !deploy ~}
# STACKIT Landing Zone: trust these claims

This run created nothing. It reports the workload identity this definition deploys as, so you can
trust it on a STACKIT service account before the landing zone is built.

## 1. Pick or create a service account

Any STACKIT service account with `resource-manager.admin` on your organization will do. It is the
identity every run of this landing zone acts as.

## 2. Register a federated identity provider on it

In the STACKIT portal, add a federated identity provider to that service account with exactly these
values:

| Claim | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Issuer** | `${wif_issuer}` |
| **Audience** (`aud` equals) | `${wif_audience}` |
| **Subject** (`sub` equals) | `${wif_subject}` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The subject names *this* definition version. Trusting a different definition, or a different
> workspace, authenticates nothing.

## 3. Run again as that account

Set **Stage** to `deploy` and fill in the **STACKIT Service Account Email** field that appears with
the account you just trusted. The next run builds the landing zone with no key stored anywhere.
%{ else ~}
# STACKIT Landing Zone: **${platform_identifier}**

%{ if playground_mode ~}
> **Playground mode.** The platform identifier carries a random suffix so this deployment does not
> occupy a name for good, and nothing here is protected against deletion. Do not publish this
> platform or the building block definitions it registered to other workspaces. Redeploy with
> `playground_mode` set to false for a platform that is actually used.

%{ endif ~}
## Details

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Organization** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${organization_url}) (`${organization_id}`) |
| **Landing Zone Folder** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${lz_folder_url}) (`${lz_folder_container_id}`) |
| **Foundation Project** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${foundation_project_url}) (`${foundation_project_id}`) |
| **Foundation Project Service Account** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${service_account_url}) (`${service_account_email}`) |
%{ if network_enabled ~}
| **Hub Network Area** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${network_area_url}) (`${network_area_id}`) |
| **Hub Network Area Building Block** | @buildingblock[${network_area_hub_uuid}] |
| **Networked Landing Zone** | `${networked_landingzone_name}` |
%{ endif ~}

%{ if network_enabled ~}
Hub-and-spoke networking is enabled. Application teams can order the **STACKIT Network** building
block inside their own projects in the `${networked_landingzone_name}` landing zone to get a routed
spoke network drawn from the hub's address plan.
%{ else ~}
Application teams can now request STACKIT projects against the `${platform_identifier}-default`
landing zone. Provide a `network` object to additionally enable hub-and-spoke networking.
%{ endif ~}

> [!TIP]
> ## Build a Kubernetes platform on top

The [STACKIT Kubernetes Platform](https://hub.meshcloud.io/reference-architectures/stackit-kubernetes)
reference architecture runs a full SKE developer platform on top of this landing zone. It takes one
value from this landing zone. Paste this block into its **STACKIT Landing Zone** input:

%{ if service_account_bbd_version_uuid == "" || service_account_federation_bbd_version_uuid == "" ~}
> [!WARNING]
> The block needs a released version of the **STACKIT Service Account** and the **STACKIT Service
> Account Federation** definitions, because another workspace may only order released versions.
> Release both in meshPanel, publish them to the marketplace, then run this building block again.

%{ endif ~}
```hcl
{
  platform_ref = { uuid = "${platform_ref.uuid}", kind = "${platform_ref.kind}" }
  landingzone_refs = {
%{ for key, ref in landingzone_refs ~}
    ${key} = { name = "${ref.name}", kind = "${ref.kind}" }
%{ endfor ~}
  }
  service_account_bbd_version_ref            = { uuid = "${service_account_bbd_version_uuid}" }
  service_account_federation_bbd_version_ref = { uuid = "${service_account_federation_bbd_version_uuid}" }
}
```
%{ if network_enabled ~}

Set its **Landing Zone Variant** to `networked` to run the cluster inside the hub-and-spoke network.
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}
